in August 2017 hurricane Harvey made  landfall in Southeast Texas dumping more  than a meter and a half of water on the  Houston area in four days  it was the wettest hurricane in US  history causing extensive flooding and a  hundred and twenty-five billion dollars  in damage so after the storm where did  all of that water go the Earth's crust  is surprisingly elastic it actually  sinks under the immense weight of new  water like what was released by Harvey  so it kind of behaves like a mattress in  a way so if I was to sit down on a  mattress  I would I would depress that mattress  with just because my weight right and so  I can measure my own weight based on how  much I depress that mattress to track  the water researchers use GPS satellites  to measure fluctuations and elevation of  the Earth's crust  subsidence down and up lift up and so  you could use that little measuring  stick of the kind of the the amount of  depression to figure out the weight of  the water the land around Houston sank  as much as 20 millimeters during  Hurricane Harvey with a clear pattern  left in the crust that moved across the  Gulf Coast for seven days consistent  with Harvey's position the GPS not only  allows us to figure out where that water  is and how much occurs but it allows us  to track how it dissipates how quickly  dissipates some how much dissipates and  the eight days following Harvey's  landfall 30% of the storm water was  stored on land as standing water sixty  percent of the storm water immediately  drained into the ocean in Galveston Bay  and the remaining 10 percent was lost to  evaporation it took five full weeks for  the water stored on land to dissipate  through evaporation or ground water  runoff okay we can do for Harvey that's  easy because there's so much water that  weight was so large that compression  that mattress is gonna be really easy to  see in the data so the next step is this  okay what about other smaller more  frequent hurricanes have less water  could we apply the same type of analysis  the ability to track daily changes in  the movement of water during extreme  events and help flood managers better  prepare for storms which will strike  with increased frequency and ferocity  as global warming causes the oceans  temperatures to rise if we have any hope  of weathering these future storms we  must remember that as my mom always says  all your do plan is planning to fail  [Music]
 

